/**
 * @defgroup Foundation_Scheduling Scheduling
 * @ingroup Foundation
 * @brief Cooperative task scheduling primitives.
 *
 * Scheduling provides a small synchronous scheduler driven by a
 * Foundation::Time::Clock. Tasks are checked and executed from the caller's
 * `TaskScheduler::Update()` call; the module creates no threads and performs no
 * heap allocation.
 *
 * @section FoundationSchedulingHeaders Public Headers
 *
 * | Need | Header |
 * | ---- | ------ |
 * | Entire Foundation library | `#include <Foundation.h>` |
 * | Entire Scheduling module | `#include <Foundation_Scheduling.h>` |
 * | Nested module umbrella | `#include <Foundation/Scheduling.h>` |
 * | One scheduling type | `#include <Foundation/Scheduling/<Type>.h>` |
 *
 * @section FoundationSchedulingTypes Public Types
 *
 * | Type | Role |
 * | ---- | ---- |
 * | Foundation::Scheduling::Task | Polymorphic interface queried and executed by a scheduler. |
 * | Foundation::Scheduling::PeriodicTask | Runs immediately, then whenever its tick period has elapsed. |
 * | Foundation::Scheduling::OneShotTask | Runs once at or after its trigger TimePoint. |
 * | Foundation::Scheduling::TaskScheduler | Stores non-owning task pointers and evaluates them using one Clock. |
 *
 * @section FoundationSchedulingFlow Execution Flow
 *
 * Each `Update()` obtains one TimePoint from the configured clock, visits tasks
 * in registration order, calls `ShouldRun(now)`, and calls `Run(now)` when the
 * result is true. Callbacks run inline, so a long-running callback delays every
 * task that follows it.
 *
 * @section FoundationSchedulingUsage Basic Usage
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * void Increment(void* context) {
 *     ++(*static_cast<unsigned int*>(context));
 * }
 *
 * Foundation::Scheduling::Task* storage[2] = {};
 * Foundation::Scheduling::TaskScheduler scheduler(storage, 2, &clock);
 *
 * unsigned int runs = 0;
 * Foundation::Scheduling::PeriodicTask task(Increment, &runs, Foundation::Time::Duration(10));
 * scheduler.AddTask(&task);
 * scheduler.Update();
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section FoundationSchedulingOwnership Ownership and Timing Contract
 *
 * - TaskScheduler does not own its storage array, Clock, or Task objects.
 * - PeriodicTask and OneShotTask do not own their callback context pointers.
 * - Every referenced object and storage array must outlive its consumer.
 * - A null task-storage pointer is valid only with zero capacity. `IsValid()`
 *   reports the storage invariant; invalid storage makes `AddTask()` fail and
 *   `Update()` a no-op.
 * - A null Clock is allowed and disables `Update()` without making task storage
 *   invalid.
 * - A null callback context is allowed and is forwarded unchanged when the
 *   callback itself is non-null. The callback decides whether null is useful.
 * - Periods and trigger times are expressed in clock ticks; compatible units
 *   and clock identity are the caller's responsibility.
 * - `Clear()` forgets registered pointers but does not destroy tasks.
 *
 * @warning A raw pointer's true lifetime cannot be determined at runtime.
 * Wrapping-tick and Clock-identity behavior remain under `API-011` in
 * @ref Foundation_Workflows_PublicApiAudit.
 *
 * @section FoundationSchedulingExample Runnable Example
 *
 * `Foundation_Scheduling_TaskScheduler` demonstrates a simulated clock,
 * periodic and one-shot tasks, capacity, and repeated updates. See
 * @ref Foundation_Examples "Examples" for build and launch instructions.
 */

/**
 * @namespace Foundation::Scheduling
 * @ingroup Foundation_Scheduling
 * @brief Cooperative scheduling types driven by Foundation Time.
 */
